## Parent baseline

> **Required.** `rpi/lock.sh` refuses to lock PLAN.md without this section
> filled, and `pre-flight-final-model` refuses to ship the bundle if it is
> missing or contains only placeholders. The motivating failure mode is
> `m4.v1 agent-10`, which built on V0 (0.01293 rad/s yaw RMSE) instead of
> V1 (0.00587), and was 2.2× off the floor before any work began.

- **Baseline**: <V0 | V1 | fresh>
- **Evidence** (one line, cite numbers): <e.g. "V1 dev pooled yaw RMSE
  0.00587 (m3.v3 cohort §1.7); V0 is 0.01293. V1 is the floor unless we
  see a structural reason it's wrong.">
- **Floor we must clear**: yaw <…> rad/s, CTE <…> m.

If your candidates target different baselines (e.g. a rung-1 fresh fit vs
a rung-0 V1 refinement), list them all here with separate evidence lines.

## Selected candidates — default 3, two only if you can justify it

**The default is three candidates: one rung-0 refinement + one orthogonal
residual learner + one structurally-different (rung 1+ or platform-targeted).**

This is a deliberate change from m4.v1's "default 2" because the m4.v1
cohort showed the median agent at +56.2% yaw, basically tied with m3.v2's
+56.6% — to lift the median past V1's ceiling, the agent has to *try* a
rung-1 climb or a platform-specific attack, not just refine. The 6-way
`launch-rungs/` fan-out gives you four or more candidates for free; PLAN.md
just decides which three you commit to running through `iterate`.

The Lightning platform is the obvious headroom target. The m4.v1 cohort
ran +21% yaw on Lightning vs +55% on Mach-E/IONIQ — Lightning is where
the next structural win lives. Candidate C below defaults to "attacks
Lightning residual".

You may ship two candidates if RESEARCH.md gives a specific reason a third
would be dominated by one of the first two. Add a `## Why only two
candidates` section at the bottom in that case.

